---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
import { t } from '../i18n/en';
import { VERDICTS } from '../lib/data';
import * as S from '../lib/scoring';
const rows = (o: Record<string, number>) => Object.entries(o);
const pts = (x: number) => Math.round(x * 100);
const vname = (k: string) => t.verdicts[k] ?? k;
---
<Base title="How we score">
  <h1>How we score</h1>
  <p class="lede">The short version: we collect things leaders said or promised, check each one against official data, give it points, and average the points for each party. Below is exactly how, so anyone can check our work. Everything on this page is generated from the same code that calculates the scores (<code>scoring.ts</code>).</p>

  <h2>What each result means</h2>
  <dl>{VERDICTS.map((v) => <><dt style="margin-top:.6em"><Badge verdict={v} /></dt><dd style="margin-left:0">{t.verdictDefs[v]}</dd></>)}</dl>
  <p>Claims that turned out true are scored exactly like every other claim.</p>

  <h2>Part 1: Were their statements true?</h2>
  <p>This covers statements and claims about numbers. Each one gets points by its result, and the party's score is the average. "Fully true" is the share that were completely true. Results not in this table are not scored.</p>
  <table class="dense"><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th class="r">Points out of 100</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows(S.VERDICT_SCORES_A).map(([k, v]) => <tr><td>{vname(k)}</td><td class="r num">{pts(v)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>

  <h2>Part 2: Did they keep their promises?</h2>
  <p>This covers promises. Each one gets points by its result, and the party's score is the average. "Kept on time" is the share fully delivered by the deadline. "Can't be checked" is not scored.</p>
  <table class="dense"><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th class="r">Points out of 100</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows(S.VERDICT_SCORES_B).map(([k, v]) => <tr><td>{vname(k)}</td><td class="r num">{pts(v)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
  <p><strong>Deadline not reached.</strong> If a promise's deadline is after {S.AS_OF}, it is not scored at all: no extra credit for finishing early and no penalty for work still going on.</p>
  <p><strong>How close to the target.</strong> For promises with a number attached, we measure how much of the way from the starting point to the target was covered: (value − start) ÷ (target − start), kept between 0% and 100%. The starting point is the figure from just before the promise was made (or the earliest figure we have). We show this at the deadline and for the latest figures. Financial years like 2015-16 are treated as ending on 31 March 2016. A note is shown when units or prices don't match, or when we had to use the earliest figure as the start.</p>

  <h2>Who counts for which party</h2>
  <p>Each claim counts for the party of the person who made it: Union ministers and the Prime Minister, Chief Ministers, and party office-bearers.</p>
  <table class="dense"><thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Claims from</th><th>Rankings measured</th></tr></thead><tbody>{S.PARTIES.map((p) => <tr><td>{p.label}</td><td>{p.claimEra}</td><td>{p.indexFrom} → {p.indexTo ?? 'latest'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
  <p><strong>How claims are picked.</strong> We look for the same number of claims for each party and each topic: 8 claims, at least 3 promises with a deadline or target, at least 3 statements or number claims, and at least 2 by Chief Ministers, chosen from the best-known and most widely reported. Researchers are told to include claims that turned out true. This makes the picking fairer, but it can't make it perfect.</p>
  <p><strong>A known imbalance.</strong> Most of India's fact-checking groups started around 2014, so Congress-era claims lean more on CAG audits, answers in Parliament and later data, and less on fact-checks from the time.</p>
  <p><strong>Comparing the two parties.</strong> For every part and for the overall score we show BJP minus Congress, with a likely range ({Math.round(S.CI_LEVEL * 100)}% confidence, worked out by re-sampling each party's claims many times). We only call a gap real when that whole range is above zero or below zero. Otherwise we say "too close to call".</p>

  <h2>Part 3: Did India's world rankings improve?</h2>
  <p>Each ranking is turned into a score out of 100, where higher is better. For a rank (say, 80th of 120 countries): 1 − (rank − 1) ÷ (number of countries − 1). For a score (say, 40 out of 100): score ÷ maximum, or the score as it is when it is already on a 0 to 1 scale. If the number of countries is missing for a year, we borrow it from the nearest year that has it and show a note.</p>
  <p>For each party we compare the edition at the start of their time in power with the last edition during it. The start can be up to {S.ERA_START_SLACK} years late (shown as "starts late"); for a party no longer in power, the last edition must be within {S.ERA_END_SLACK} years of them leaving. A ranking scores {pts(S.C_IMPROVED)} if India moved up, {pts(S.C_FLAT)} if it barely changed (less than {S.C_EPS * 100} points), and {pts(S.C_DECLINED)} if it moved down. The part's score is the average. We also show the average change and the change per year, because the two periods are different lengths.</p>
  <table class="dense"><thead><tr><th>Ranking with a known problem</th><th>Problem</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody>{Object.entries(S.INDEX_FLAGS).map(([k, f]) => <tr><td class="num">{k}</td><td>{f.kind === 'comparability' ? 'hard to compare across years' : 'no longer published'}</td><td>{f.text}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
  <p>The "skip rankings with known problems" switch leaves these out of Part 3 and the overall score.</p>

  <h2>Topics</h2>
  <p>Each claim is counted only under its first (main) topic.</p>
  <table class="dense"><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Claim tags</th><th>Rankings</th></tr></thead><tbody>{S.DOMAINS.map((d) => <tr><td>{d.name}</td><td>{d.topics.join(', ')}</td><td>{d.indices.join(', ') || '—'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>

  <h2>The overall score</h2>
  <p>Overall = (weight 1 × Part 1 + weight 2 × Part 2 + weight 3 × Part 3) ÷ (sum of the weights)</p>
  <p>By default the three parts count {pts(S.DEFAULT_WEIGHTS.A)}%, {pts(S.DEFAULT_WEIGHTS.B)}% and {pts(S.DEFAULT_WEIGHTS.C)}%. You can change this with the sliders on the scoreboard. A part with nothing to score is left out and the others are scaled up to fill in.</p>

  <h2>How sure are the numbers?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The "likely" ranges are {Math.round(S.CI_LEVEL * 100)}% ranges from re-sampling the claims {S.BOOTSTRAP_N} times (statisticians call this a bootstrap). We use a fixed starting point ({S.SEED}) so every rebuild gives the same numbers.</li>
    <li>When something has fewer than {S.MIN_N} claims, we show "too few to score" instead of a number.</li>
    <li>Every score says how many claims it is based on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Background entries</h2>
  <p>Some entries are marked "background only". They have their own page but are left out of every score. They may appear as extra context on a related claim.</p>

  <h2>Sources and checking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every entry needs proof the person really said it (a video or written record) and at least two sources for the result, preferring government data, answers in Parliament, CAG audits and the RBI.</li>
    <li>Quotes are marked "not word for word" unless an original source has the exact words.</li>
    <li>If the government has responded, we include its response.</li>
    <li>An entry isn't final until two people have checked the quote, the sources and the result. Until then it is marked "not checked yet". Right now every entry is unchecked, so all scores are early results.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Mistakes are fixed in the data files, with the date and a note saying what changed. Scores are recalculated every time the site is rebuilt.</p>
</Base>
